// Practice quiz: Constructors
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/constructors

::o1 initialize fields::class Item \{ String name; int qty; Item(String name, int qty) \{ ... \} \} Which body sets both fields? {
=this.name \= name; this.qty \= qty;#Correct. this. names the field, so each parameter is copied into its field.
~name \= name; qty \= qty;#Not quite. Each name means the parameter here, so the parameters are assigned to themselves and the fields stay at their defaults.
~return new Item(name, qty);#Not quite. A constructor does not return a value, and this would call itself again.
~Item.name \= name; Item.qty \= qty;#Not quite. Item.name would need static fields; these fields belong to each object.
}

::o1 constructor shape::What makes a declaration a constructor rather than a method? {
=It has the same name as its class and no return type#Correct. Those two features are what the compiler uses to recognize a constructor.
~It has the return type void#Not quite. void Item() is an ordinary method that happens to share the class name.
~It is marked static#Not quite. Constructors are never static; they set up a new object.
~It can have any name if it is called first#Not quite. A constructor must have its class name.
}

::o2 this dot::In a constructor, this.price \= price; runs. What does this.price refer to? {
=The price field of the object being created#Correct. this is the object under construction, so this.price is its field.
~The price parameter#Not quite. The plain name price is the parameter; this.price is the field.
~A static field shared by every object#Not quite. this always means the current object, never the class.
~The price field of the previously created object#Not quite. Each constructor call works on its own new object.
}

::o2 this call::class Seat \{ Seat(int row, int col) \{ ... \} Seat(int row) \{ this(row, 1); \} \} What does this(row, 1) do? {
=Runs the two-parameter constructor on the same new object#Correct. this(...) chains to another constructor of the same class, so the setup code is written once.
~Creates a second Seat object#Not quite. No second object is made; both constructors work on one object.
~Calls a method named this#Not quite. this(...) is constructor-chaining syntax, not a method call.
~Causes endless recursion#Not quite. It calls a different constructor, the one with two parameters, so the chain ends.
}

::o3 no constructor::class Lamp \{ boolean on; \} has no constructor written. Does new Lamp() compile? {
=Yes; the compiler supplies a default constructor with no parameters#Correct. When a class declares no constructor, the compiler adds an empty no-argument one.
~No; every class must declare a constructor#Not quite. Without any constructor, the compiler supplies the default one.
~No; on must be given a value first#Not quite. Fields get defaults, so on starts as false.
~Yes, but on is left without any value#Not quite. The default constructor leaves on at its default, false.
}

::o3 lost default::class Room \{ int number; Room(int number) \{ this.number \= number; \} \} Does new Room() compile? {
=No; declaring any constructor stops the compiler supplying the default one#Correct. The only constructor needs an int, so a call with no arguments does not match.
~Yes; the default constructor is always there#Not quite. The default is supplied only when the class declares no constructor at all.
~Yes, and number is set to 0#Not quite. No matching constructor exists, so nothing runs.
~It compiles but throws an exception when run#Not quite. This is caught by the compiler, before the program runs.
}
